Title:
RPC interfaces dump August 2018 (rpc_dump_august.txt)

Type:
Documentation / Other (data dump / enumeration output)

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This gist is a large dump of Microsoft Windows RPC interfaces and their registered endpoints as observed on a Windows system around August 2018.  
- It maps processes/services (e.g., `services.exe`, `lsass.exe`, `spoolsv.exe`, `svchost.exe` groups) to RPC interface UUIDs, versions, procedure names/ordinals, and endpoint bindings (e.g., `ncalrpc`, `ncacn_np` named pipes, `ncacn_ip_tcp` ports like 135).  
- The data is useful for identifying exposed RPC attack surface, correlating undocumented interfaces to hosting binaries/DLLs, and guiding reverse engineering or fuzzing efforts.  
- It also highlights practical connection points such as well-known pipes (`\\pipe\\ntsvcs`, `\\pipe\\lsass`, `\\pipe\\spoolss`, `\\pipe\\wkssvc`, `\\pipe\\srvsvc`) and local RPC endpoints used for privileged service communication.  
- Red teams can use it to discover candidate lateral movement/priv-esc primitives or coercion vectors; blue teams can use it to baseline expected RPC exposure and hunt anomalous endpoints.  
- Interesting because it provides a concrete, process-scoped view of RPC interface registration rather than only protocol documentation, enabling faster targeting and detection engineering.

Technical Focus:
- Windows RPC interface UUIDs and versioning
- Endpoint bindings: LRPC (`ncalrpc`), Named Pipes (`ncacn_np`), TCP (`ncacn_ip_tcp`)
- Service-to-binary/DLL RPC hosting relationships (svchost service groups, COM/RPCSS)
- High-value RPC surfaces (LSA/SAMR/SCM/Spooler/EventLog)
- RPC procedure enumeration (opnums, method names)
- Attack surface mapping / RPC reconnaissance

Use Cases:
- Build an RPC attack-surface inventory for a Windows host image/baseline
- Prioritize RPC interfaces for reverse engineering and fuzzing (by service criticality/exposure)
- Identify reachable endpoints for lateral movement tooling (pipes/ports) and coercion research
- Create defensive allowlists/monitoring for “expected” RPC endpoints per role
- Correlate EDR telemetry to specific RPC servers (process + interface UUID)

Keywords:
Windows RPC, MSRPC, DCE/RPC, UUID, interface GUID, opnum, endpoint mapper, RpcSs, rpcrt4.dll, ncalrpc, ncacn_np, ncacn_ip_tcp, named pipe, \\pipe\\epmapper, port 135, services.exe, lsass.exe, samsrv.dll, lsasrv.dll, spoolsv.exe, wevtsvc.dll, wkssvc.dll, srvsvc.dll, SCM, SAMR, LSA, COM, OLE, svchost.exe